data.content:
Here are the improved MSTest assertions:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    Assert.AreNotEqual(0, users.Count);                          // instead of Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0)
    CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull(users);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));                   // no built-in predicate assert; this one is fine
    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count());                           // instead of Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3)
    var admin = users.Single(u => u.Role == "Admin");
    Assert.IsNotNull(admin);                                     // instead of Assert.IsTrue(admin != null)
}
```

**Key replacements:**

| Original | Better assertion |
|----------|-----------------|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count > 0)` | `Assert.AreNotEqual(0, x.Count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(x.Count() == 3)` | `Assert.AreEqual(3, x.Count())` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(obj != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(obj)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(collection.All(predicate))` | Keep as-is (MSTest has no `CollectionAssert.AllSatisfy`) |

Using specific assertions gives you much better failure messages — e.g., `Assert.AreEqual` tells you the expected vs. actual value, while `Assert.IsTrue` just says "expected true, got false."
